  "Route: the owned files handbook/parts/p15/probability-spaces-and-random-variables-w02c-p15-001.tex and handbook/annex/p15/probability-spaces-and-random-variables-w02c-p15-001.tex are not yet input in handbook/parts/p15.tex or handbook/annex/p15.tex (packet insertion after line 41 and line 40 respectively).",
  "Undefined notation on the current route: int f(x,y) dx and int_A f(x,y) dx are used in handbook/parts/p15/conditional-expectation.tex lines 229-234 (thm:p15-conditional-density-formulas) and handbook/parts/p15/regular-conditional-laws.tex lines 114-119 (thm:p15-density-conditional-kernel), and int_0^1 f(x) dx in handbook/annex/p15/radon-nikodym.tex lines 628-632; no routed chapter before them defines the dx or interval-limit notation. def:p15-w02c-p15-001-dx-notation (owned body lines 68-165) supplies it once routed.",
  "handbook/annex/p15/radon-nikodym.tex lines 628-632 and 653-659 obtain the integrals of 2x-1, the finite mass 'log n' of [1/n,1) and the divergence on (0,a) by direct one-variable integration (ordinary calculus, licensed on compact intervals by annex:p15.fubini-failures, product-measures annex lines 369-389); the calculus-free proofs thm:p15-w02c-p15-001-affine-integral, -signed-density-values and -reciprocal-density are not cross-referenced from that readonly annex.",
  "handbook/parts/p15/integration-expectation.tex lines 249-251 give the divergence of sum 1/(k+1) in one sentence; the full grouping argument is written in the owned annex lines 375-382.",
  "The owned files rely on labels in other unrouted w02c packet files (thm:p15-w02c-p15-002-sigma-finite-uniqueness and def:p15-w02c-p15-002-uniqueness-failure in probability-spaces-and-random-variables-w02c-p15-002.tex; thm:p15-w02c-p15-005-* in measure-integration-and-product-constructions-w02c-p15-005.tex; thm:p15-w02c-p15-003-block-integrals). The proofs of thm:p15-w02c-p15-001-lebesgue-rectangles and part (1) of thm:p15-w02c-p15-001-stieltjes-notation rest on the w02c-p15-002 uniqueness theorem, so routing must include that file.",
  "Elementary one-variable calculus (mean value theorem, fundamental theorem, integration by parts, arctan antiderivative) is used as background in handbook/annex/p15/convolution-gamma-normal.tex (lines 7-12, 232-265, 423-430, 450-461) and handbook/annex/p15/product-measures.tex lines 369-389. Recorded for the reviewer; not treated as a coverage gap."
